{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And he shall take off his garments, etc.</b> Rashi explained: this is not an obligation but teaches you proper conduct, etc. For it was difficult for Rashi, for after all in the portion of אַחֲרֵי מוֹת (Leviticus 16:23) it says ״וּפָשַׁט אֶת בִּגְדֵי הַבָּד״, and there it does not say 'his garments,' and the Sages learned (Sifra, Aharei 13) that they do not come from his own property but from the property of the sanctuary. If so, why does it call them here 'his garments' (בְּגָדָיו), when they are not his? Rather, it means to say that he should treat these sacred garments in the manner of the whole world, just as he would treat them were they his garments in truth. For just as, had they been his, he would certainly remove them at a time when he is performing some task that is contemptible and repulsive, and would put on lesser garments, so too he should do even with these sacred garments. And infer from this and from that: just as his own garments are optional, so too these are optional — for if not, why would the verse suspend them on 'his garments' (בְּגָדָיו)?"], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:35:45.113361517Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}